    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • XPENG Reveals G9L at the 2026 Paris Motor Show, Dubbed As the Ultimate \"Large SUV for the AI Era\"\nCheaper Volkswagen ID. Cross Is Now Available With a 37 kWh Battery Pack; This Is How Much It Costs\n6 photos\nPhoto: Volkswagen AG\nVolkswagen continues to democratize its electrified and all-electric range, introduces a more affordable version of the ID. Cross with a smaller 37-kWh pack at home in Germany, starting from €27,995, which is the equivalent of a little over $31k at the current exchange rate.\nThe German automaker is in deep trouble – we all know that. But the company has actually timed its soft EV reboot quite well, as the Old Continent is increasingly leaning toward EV models as an alternative to escape the high oil prices of recent months. The only problem is that the Chinese are also here to try and take advantage of the new status quo.\nVolkswagen is ready to open a new chapter in its EV book, though, and has already revealed the all-new or refreshed VW ID.3 Neo, ID. Polo, ID. Polo GTI, ID. Cross, plus the newly unveiled ID. Tiguan . The latter is trying to make us forget about the rather appalling and boring ID.4 with a family-oriented proposal, but the Old Continent will also take a look at the ID. Cross, which is smaller and nimbler across town.\nAnyway, to make it even more enticing, Volkswagen is now starting pre-sales for the new ID. Cross with a smaller 37-kWh battery pack. The electric subcompact crossover SUV, which is basically the EV equivalent of the VW T-Cross model, has two battery options for the powertrain: 37-kWh LFP or 52-kWh NMC battery.\nThe smaller version “is primarily aimed at customers who mainly drive in urban traffic and on typical commuting routes and at the same time value an attractive entry-level price” summarizes VW's offer, as pricing for the Trend model at home in Germany kicks off at less than €28k. The smallest battery pack is available with the 85 kW (116 ps or 114 hp) and 99 kW (135 ps or 133 hp) drive systems, as well as the Trend, Life, and Style equipment lines.\nInitially, only the larger 52 kWh battery pack was available to order, but now the lower purchase and operating costs model is also up for grabs, announced with a WLTP range of up to 324 km (201 miles) and a DC rapid charging system, allowing a recharge from 10 to 80 per cent in around 23 minutes, as standard. It can even drive on highways, as the top speed is 160 kph or 99 mph.\nRight off the bat, the entry-level Trend version comes with standard perks like front and rear Park Distance Control and smartphone integration via “App Connect”, while Life and Style equipment lines bring comfort and technology features from higher classes like “Connected Travel Assist” or a Harman Kardon sound system, among others. So, what do you think - yay or nay?\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVolkswagen ID. Cross VW Volkswagen id. cross 37 kWh EV crossover SUV pricing Germany\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT 5 Most Iconic Muscle Cars Introduced During the 1965 Model Year",
